import * as V from './ch06.ui.shared.js';
export default { collection: 'dist', chapter: 6, items: [
  { num: '6.6', kind: 'Algorithm', name: 'Authenticated Byzantine agreement (Dolev–Strong)',
    statement: String.raw`Dolev–Strong solves **designated-sender Byzantine broadcast** in a synchronous complete network with at most $f<n$ faulty processes and an established public-key infrastructure. All correct processes know the same verification key for each identity. Assume ideal unforgeability: nobody can produce an unissued signature of a correct process. Real cryptographic security replaces this idealization by a negligible failure probability against the stated bounded adversary.
For a binary input, keep a set of accepted values. The sender signs its input. In receive round $r=1,\ldots,f+1$, accept a value only with a valid chain of exactly $r$ distinct signers beginning with the sender. On first accepting a value, append one's own signature and forward to everyone for the next round, unless this is the last round. At the deadline, output the sole accepted value if the set has size one, otherwise output the public default 0. Bind every signature to the protocol, session, value and preceding chain.`,
    idea: String.raw`An honest sender signs only its input for this session, so unforgeability prevents any other value from entering a correct process's set. That proves sender validity.
For agreement, suppose a correct process first accepts a value before the last round. It cannot already occur as a signer in that chain: an earlier honest signature would mean it had accepted the value earlier. It appends its signature and sends the extended chain to all correct recipients, who accept it by the next round if they have not already done so.
If a correct process first accepts a value in the last round, the $f+1$ distinct signers include an honest one. That signer previously accepted and forwarded the same value to everyone, so all correct processes have it by the deadline. All correct accepted sets are therefore equal, giving equal decisions.
The round-to-chain-length rule is essential. If a faulty sender can inject a short old chain into just one recipient at the final deadline, that recipient may acquire a second value too late to propagate it. Likewise stopping one round early permits a chain containing only the $f$ traitors to create disagreement.
Strong unanimity consensus cannot tolerate $f\ge n/2$, even with signatures: split the identities into two nonempty groups of at most $f$, and consider a fault-free run with opposite inputs in the two groups. Each group has the same view as a run where the other group is faulty but simulates its fault-free behavior. Unanimity validity would force each honest group to decide its own input, contradicting agreement in the fault-free run.`,
    caption: String.raw`Choose a fault budget and an equivocation or delayed-message attack. Inspect each round's accepted sets and rejection reasons. Turn off the chain-length rule or stop early to expose the late-message failure.`,
    scope: String.raw`The traditional name is retained, but the all-but-one guarantee is for broadcast. Consensus with strong honest-unanimity validity still requires an honest majority; broadcasting every proposal and taking the majority works only with that additional premise. The signature ledger is an ideal interface, not an implemented cryptosystem. Only the ideal signature properties stated here are used.`,
    deps: ['dist-6.1'], tags: ['byzantine', 'signatures'], credit: 'Dolev & Strong, 1983', refs: ['University of Washington CSE422wi24, chapter 3, The Dolev-Strong Protocol, §3.4 and both consistency lemmas; original DS83 body not read', 'Jonathan Katz (2021), Broadcast and Byzantine Agreement, §§1,4, for definitions and a separate optimized all-but-one formulation'], links: { source: 'https://homes.cs.washington.edu/~jrl/cse422wi24/notes/blockchain_3.pdf' }, viz: V.signedWidget },
  { num: '6.7', kind: 'Concept', name: 'Byzantine quorums',
    statement: String.raw`For subsets $Q_1,Q_2$ of $n$ processes,
$$|Q_1\cap Q_2|\ge |Q_1|+|Q_2|-n.$$
If at most $f$ identities are faulty, their intersection must contain an honest identity whenever $|Q_1|+|Q_2|>n+f$. In particular, with $n=3f+1$, two quorums of size $2f+1$ intersect in at least $f+1$ identities, including an honest one.
This is a set-theoretic tool for protocol proofs. A protocol must additionally specify what an honest member may attest to, and what evidence it preserves across views.`,
    idea: String.raw`Count the union: $|Q_1\cup Q_2|\le n$. Rearranging inclusion–exclusion gives the lower bound, and deleting at most $f$ faulty members leaves at least one honest member under the strict inequality. If honest processes never sign conflicting votes for the same phase, view and sequence, two certificates whose intersection is forced to contain an honest signer cannot certify conflicting values in that context.
A new view is a different context. Intersection alone does not forbid changing a vote there; a safe carry-forward rule must connect old evidence to new proposals. Conversely, using a quorum so large that it cannot be assembled from the $n-f$ responsive correct processes can prevent progress. Safety and availability impose different constraints.`,
    caption: String.raw`Edit both quorum sets and the faulty set. The overlap diagram counts actual honest members and compares them with the worst-case guarantee. Try two simple majorities whose entire overlap is faulty.`,
    scope: String.raw`Membership lists contain distinct authenticated identities. The scene proves the finite counting identity, not a complete consensus protocol or an assumption that every member is online.`,
    deps: ['dist-6.2'], tags: ['quorums', 'byzantine'], refs: ['Castro–Liskov (1999), Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance, §4; direct inclusion–exclusion proof supplied'], links: { source: 'https://www.usenix.org/legacy/publications/library/proceedings/osdi99/full_papers/castro/castro_html/castro.html' }, viz: V.quorumWidget },
  { num: '6.8', kind: 'Algorithm', name: 'Practical Byzantine fault tolerance (PBFT)',
    statement: String.raw`PBFT replicates a deterministic service with $n=3f+1$ replicas and at most $f$ Byzantine faults. Messages authenticate their sender and bind the request digest, view and sequence number. In a normal view:
(a) The primary sends a pre-prepare assigning the request to a sequence number.
(b) Backups accept at most one value for that view/sequence and multicast prepares. A replica is prepared after a matching pre-prepare and prepares from $2f$ distinct backups.
(c) A prepared replica multicasts commit. It commits locally only when prepared and holding matching commits from $2f+1$ distinct replicas, then executes in sequence order.
A client waits for $f+1$ matching replies from distinct replicas. Safety does not require timely delivery. Liveness additionally needs eventual sufficiently good timing, a correct primary and enough responsive replicas; it is not guaranteed in arbitrary asynchronous executions.`,
    idea: String.raw`The prepare stage prevents conflicting prepared certificates in one view. If the primary is honest it issues only one value. If the primary is faulty, two sets of $2f$ backup prepares, drawn from $3f$ backups, intersect in at least $f$ backups; at most $f-1$ backups can then be faulty. An honest backup would have to prepare both values, which it will not do.
The commit stage ensures that a locally committed value is prepared at at least $f+1$ honest replicas. Any $2f+1$ view-change reports intersect that honest set. A valid new view carries prepared certificates and, for each relevant sequence, selects the value with highest prepared view reported. Induction over views preserves a committed value: once preserved, no conflicting later prepared certificate can be formed. In the complete protocol, stable checkpoints bound the log and missing slots inside the transferred range are filled with null requests.
The finite example has four replicas, one sequence, view 0 and one change to view 1. Correct view-change reports carry their actual prepared certificates; a faulty reporter may omit its own. A proposed conflicting replacement is rejected when the carried certificate fixes the value. This exposes why a bare majority or a leader change without evidence is insufficient.`,
    caption: String.raw`Edit the primary's equivocation, a faulty backup's votes and the three view-change reporters. Step through preparation, local commitment and the new primary. Try proposing a replacement that contradicts a carried certificate.`,
    scope: String.raw`The model uses ideal certificates verifiable by every replica and two distinguishable request digests. A pairwise MAC tag alone is not a transferable signature. It omits client retries, checkpoint transfer, pipelining, full recovery and timer/backoff behavior. A temporarily isolated receiver misses others' prepare/commit messages in view 0 and reconnects for view 1. A fresh slot with no prepared evidence may take a fresh request; this example has no higher slot requiring a null-filled gap. It is not a production PBFT implementation.`,
    deps: ['dist-6.7', 'dist-5.12', 'crypto-5.1'], tags: ['byzantine', 'replication'], credit: 'Castro & Liskov, 1999', refs: ['Castro–Liskov (1999), Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance, publisher HTML §§2–4', 'DIST 5.12 for partial-synchrony safety/progress separation, not for the Byzantine quorum threshold'], links: { source: 'https://www.usenix.org/legacy/publications/library/proceedings/osdi99/full_papers/castro/castro_html/castro.html' }, viz: V.pbftWidget },
  { num: '6.9', kind: 'Model', name: 'Nakamoto consensus: proof of work and the longest chain',
    statement: String.raw`Proof of work lets participants weigh competing histories by accumulated valid computational work. A block refers to its parent and commits to transactions; a Merkle root can provide the transaction commitment. Nodes validate blocks and prefer the valid chain with the most accumulated work. Counting blocks is equivalent only when each block contributes the same work.
In an idealized race, independent block discoveries belong to an attacker with probability $q$ and to honest miners with probability $p=1-q$. Starting $z$ blocks behind, and counting a tie as catch-up, the attacker's eventual catch-up probability is
$$\begin{cases}(q/p)^z,&q<p,\\1,&q\ge p,\end{cases}$$
with probability 1 at $z=0$. This fixed-deficit calculation differs from the probability after waiting for $z$ honest confirmations while the attacker mines in parallel.`,
    idea: String.raw`The deficit makes a nearest-neighbor random walk: an honest discovery adds one, an attacker discovery subtracts one, and zero is absorbing. For $q<p$, solve the two-boundary hitting problem on $0,\ldots,M$ and let $M$ grow; the probability of ever hitting zero is $(q/p)^z$. For nonpositive drift away from zero, eventual hitting has probability one. Finite-horizon dynamic programming instead sums only paths that have caught up within the selected number of discoveries.
For confirmations, the paper approximates the time to $z$ honest blocks by its mean and treats attacker progress $K$ as Poisson with mean $zq/p$. It averages catch-up probability over the remaining deficit. The separate exact stopping-time model gives $K$ a negative-binomial distribution: the number of attacker discoveries before the $z$th honest discovery. For $z\ge1$ and $q<p$, splitting at $K=z$ gives
$$2\Pr[\operatorname{Bin}(2z-1,q)\ge z].$$
To see the factor two, the weighted terms for $k<z$ are $\binom{z+k-1}{k}q^z p^k$. Their sum is the probability that the attacker wins a race to $z$ discoveries, which equals the tail probability $\Pr[K\ge z]$. The two contributions are equal. This exact comparison is a supplied derivation of the ideal stopping-time model, not a formula claimed from the original paper.
Collision resistance binds block and transaction contents; it alone does not imply uniform unpredictable mining trials. The independent-discovery assumption and eventual network behavior are separate premises.`,
    caption: String.raw`Edit a sequence of honest blocks, private attacker blocks and releases to grow a real parent-linked tree. Separately vary hash share and deficit to compare fixed-deficit catch-up, a finite horizon, the paper's Poisson approximation and the exact stopping-time model.`,
    scope: String.raw`The tree uses one unit of work per block, immediate honest propagation and an explicit keep-current-tip tie rule. A tie in the probability calculation is counted as catch-up, not guaranteed transaction reversal. Neither toy model establishes common-prefix security under arbitrary delays or selfish mining, nor does it supply a financial or confirmation-policy recommendation. Tiny probabilities are computed and displayed in log space.`,
    deps: ['dist-6.2', 'crypto-5.6', 'crypto-5.14'], tags: ['proof-of-work', 'probability'], credit: 'Nakamoto, 2008', refs: ['Satoshi Nakamoto (2008), Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System, §§3–6,8,11', 'Independent derivation supplied for negative-binomial stopping-time comparison'], links: { source: 'https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf' }, viz: V.miningWidget },
  { num: '6.10', kind: 'Paper', name: 'The Byzantine Generals Problem',
    paper: { authors: 'Leslie Lamport, Robert Shostak and Marshall Pease', year: 1982, title: 'The Byzantine Generals Problem', venue: 'ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems 4 (3): 382–401', doi: '10.1145/357172.357176' },
    introduces: ['dist-6.1'],
    statement: String.raw`Lamport, Shostak and Pease formulated Byzantine broadcast through loyal generals and traitors, separated oral-message and signed-message assumptions, and proved recursive protocols under each model. Their oral-message algorithm OM($f$) works with $n>3f$; their signed-message construction tolerates arbitrary numbers of traitors under its authentication and timing assumptions.
Figures 1–2 illustrate the three-general obstruction, but the paper explicitly warns that this illustration is not a rigorous impossibility proof. Figures 3–4 show how four generals handle one traitor with OM(1); Figure 5 shows how transferable signatures change the three-general example.`,
    idea: String.raw`For OM(1), the commander sends an order to every lieutenant. Each lieutenant forwards its received order to every other lieutenant. Each correct lieutenant then takes the majority of one report per lieutenant, including its own direct order. If the commander is honest, every correct lieutenant reports the same order and the single faulty lieutenant cannot defeat the majority among at least three lieutenants. If the commander is faulty, all lieutenants are honest: they assemble exactly the same vector of the commander's possibly different orders and take the same majority.
With only two lieutenants this majority protection can fail: a loyal commander's 1 and a traitor's false 0 report tie, yielding default 0. Conversely direct obedience to an equivocating commander can break agreement. Those are counterexamples to the selected rules; the chapter's separate indistinguishability theorem excludes every oral-message protocol under the stated bound.
The paper also studies missing communication paths. Its specialized “regular set of neighbors” is a disjoint-path condition, not merely vertex degree. Its signed-message proof assumes unforgeability; a later random-malfunction signature illustration is not a construction secure against malicious forgery.`,
    caption: String.raw`Reconstruct the oral-message cases with three or four generals. Edit direct orders and false relays, step from sending to forwarding to decision, and compare each honest lieutenant's actual report vector.`,
    scope: String.raw`The displayed 0/1 experiment is a newly drawn reconstruction of OM(1), with default 0. It is not a reproduction of the original figures or a proof of impossibility from one failed execution.`,
    deps: ['dist-1.3', 'dist-5.1'], tags: ['byzantine', 'historical-paper'], refs: ['Lamport–Shostak–Pease (1982), complete paper, especially pp. 384–390,392,394; original figures visually inspected'], links: { source: 'https://lamport.azurewebsites.net/pubs/byz.pdf' }, viz: V.paperWidget },
] };
